Chapter: UdpFrameGuard
  cites Net chapter UdpIO
  cites Net chapter Udp
  cites Net chapter Ethernet
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 We say:

Section: Wire Fixtures
  srv-mac : List Integer = [82, 84, 0, 18, 52, 86]
  cli-mac : List Integer = [82, 85, 10, 0, 2, 2]
  srv-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 15]
  cli-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 100]

  sk : UdpSocket = udp-io-open srv-mac srv-ip 68 cli-ip 67

  good-frame : List Integer =
    udp-build-frame srv-mac cli-mac cli-ip srv-ip 67 68 [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]

  other-port-frame : List Integer =
    udp-build-frame srv-mac cli-mac cli-ip srv-ip 67 69 [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]

  take : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  take (xs) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else take xs (i + 1) n (list-push acc (list-at xs i))

  corrupt-at : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  corrupt-at (bytes) (i) = list-set-at bytes i (bit-xor (list-at bytes i) 1)

Section: The Arms

 cut-frame is the one that used to take the guest down. It is a truncated
 receive: the IP header arrived intact and still claims the bytes that were
 lost, so the checksum holds and only the length disagrees.

 runt-frame is shorter than an IP header, which is what makes `ip-proto`
 index byte 9 of a list that does not have one.

 bad-hdr-frame flips a byte inside the twenty the header checksum covers.

 other-port-frame is the control that stops a guard which refuses everything
 from passing alongside good-frame: it is well formed, and it must be refused
 for a reason that is not the guard.

  cut-frame : List Integer = take good-frame 0 (list-length good-frame - 6) []
  runt-frame : List Integer = take good-frame 0 24 []
  bad-hdr-frame : List Integer = corrupt-at good-frame 26

 The UDP header starts at 34: 14 of Ethernet and 20 of IP. Its length field is
 at 38 and its checksum at 40, and the payload begins at 42.

 no-cksum-frame is the one that must be ACCEPTED. A stored zero means "not
 computed" on IPv4 UDP, so refusing it would refuse every peer that declines to
 compute one. It is the control against a checker that has become strict rather
 than correct, and it is the arm most likely to be broken by a future edit.

  zero-at : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  zero-at (bytes) (i) = list-set-at bytes i 0

  bad-cksum-frame : List Integer = corrupt-at good-frame 42
  bad-len-frame : List Integer = corrupt-at good-frame 39
  no-cksum-frame : List Integer = zero-at (zero-at (corrupt-at good-frame 42) 40) 41

Section: Entry
  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("good-len=" & show (list-length good-frame))
    print-line-uni ("good-mine=" & show (udp-io-mine sk good-frame))
    print-line-uni ("good-payload=" & show (list-length (udp-io-payload good-frame)))
    print-line-uni ("other-port-mine=" & show (udp-io-mine sk other-port-frame))
    print-line-uni ("cut-mine=" & show (udp-io-mine sk cut-frame))
    print-line-uni ("runt-mine=" & show (udp-io-mine sk runt-frame))
    print-line-uni ("bad-hdr-mine=" & show (udp-io-mine sk bad-hdr-frame))
    print-line-uni ("good-cksum-nonzero=" & show (read-be16 good-frame 40 /= 0))
    print-line-uni ("bad-cksum-mine=" & show (udp-io-mine sk bad-cksum-frame))
    print-line-uni ("bad-len-mine=" & show (udp-io-mine sk bad-len-frame))
    print-line-uni ("no-cksum-mine=" & show (udp-io-mine sk no-cksum-frame))
    print-line-uni ("survived")
  end
